      "jrtrack-wiki-part-2": {
        "type": "jrtrack-wiki-part-2",
        "settings": {
          "page_03": "Features",
          "page_04": "<b>Rather just ask?</b> Real humans, seven days a week. <a href=\"tel:8772154741\">877-215-4741</a>"
        }
      },
      "jrtrack-wiki-part-3": {
        "type": "jrtrack-wiki-part-3",
        "settings": {
          "overview_01": "What is JrTrack?",
          "overview_02": "A standalone smartwatch, built around a contact list you control",
          "overview_03": "<em>In short</em>JrTrack has its own phone number and its own cellular connection. Your child does not need a phone. You do, for the Cosmo app, which is where everything is managed.",
          "overview_04": "It works at the bus stop, on the field, and three towns over without touching your phone's plan or your home Wi-Fi. Calls and texts come from the people you have approved, and nobody else. Location is live in the app whenever you want it.",
          "overview_05": "Two halves make the product. The watch on their wrist handles calling, texting, location, and a curated set of apps. The Cosmo app on your phone handles who can reach them, where they are, what the school day looks like, and every message.",
          "overview_06": "Built for the first solo trips",
          "overview_07": "Five named systems, all on every JrTrack",
          "overview_08": "The features below have names because they are engineered as systems, not as checkboxes. Each gets its own chapter on this page. None of them is a paid tier, and all of them ship on JrTrack Pro.",
          "overview_09": "<b><a href=\"#halogps\">HaloGPS</a></b><span>Multi-constellation location with Active Tracking, SafeZones, and Wi-Fi assist</span>",
          "overview_10": "<b><a href=\"#safecore\">SafeCore</a></b><span>SOS to every Guardian, SafeZone alerts, optional 911, approved contacts only</span>",
          "overview_11": "<b><a href=\"#texting\">TrueTexting</a></b><span>Real SMS and MMS from the watch's own number, readable in the Cosmo app</span>",
          "overview_12": "<b><a href=\"#schoolfi\">School-Fi</a></b><span>Location-triggered distraction-free mode for any school campus in America</span>",
          "overview_13": "<b><a href=\"#mobile\">Cosmo Mobile</a></b><span>FlexSIM eSIM that roams across major towers for 4G LTE coverage</span>",
          "overview_14": "Who it is for",
          "overview_15": "<em>In short</em>Kids ages 6 to 12. The real test is not the birthday but whether they regularly go somewhere without you.",
          "overview_16": "<strong>Ages 6 to 7.</strong> Handoffs. Daycare pickup, a grandparent's car, the walk from the school door to the corner. Arrival alerts and a call button carry the load.",
          "overview_17": "<strong>Ages 8 to 10.</strong> The sweet spot. Bike rides, walking home, staying after for practice, a friend's house two streets over. Texting and location both get used daily.",
          "overview_18": "<strong>Ages 11 to 12.</strong> Works well when the answer to a phone is not yet. The keyboard on JrTrack Pro matters most here.",
          "overview_19": "The watch starts with the basics and lets you unlock more as they are ready. Apps, 911, and Buddies are all switches you flip, not defaults.",
          "overview_20": "Starts with the basics, grows from there",
          "halogps_01": "Feature 01",
          "halogps_02": "HaloGPS",
          "halogps_03": "Engineered to find them, every time. Multi-constellation GPS, Wi-Fi assist, and live updates every 5 to 10 seconds when you want to watch them move.",
          "halogps_04": "<b>115</b><span>satellite signals</span>",
          "halogps_05": "<b>5 to 10 s</b><span>Active Tracking</span>",
          "halogps_06": "<b>92%</b><span>faster first lock<sup>4</sup></span>",
          "halogps_07": "<b>Wi-Fi</b><span>assist indoors</span>",
          "halogps_08": "How it finds them",
          "halogps_09": "<em>In short</em>The watch listens to 115 satellite signals across multiple constellations at once, instead of one network. Losing a few signals to a rooftop or a tree line does not cost the fix.",
          "halogps_10": "An older single-network GPS chip needs a clear view of a handful of satellites. Downtown, under trees, or in a school corridor, that is a hard ask, and the result is the wandering dot every parent of a first-generation kids watch has complained about.",
          "halogps_11": "HaloGPS pulls from several satellite systems simultaneously, so there are always more signals than it needs. Indoors, Wi-Fi assist uses nearby networks as extra reference points to keep the dot on the right building. In Cosmo lab testing, first lock came about 92 percent faster than single-constellation GPS.<sup>4</sup>",
          "halogps_12": "Two kids, one map, in the Cosmo app",
          "halogps_13": "Active Tracking",
          "halogps_14": "<em>In short</em>Normally the watch checks in on a schedule you set. Tap Active Tracking and it updates every 5 to 10 seconds, for the twenty minutes you actually need it.",
          "halogps_15": "This is the mode for the walk home, the bike ride to practice, or the ten minutes after a \"where are you?\" text goes unanswered. The map moves with them.",
          "halogps_16": "It is also the single biggest lever on battery life, which is why it is a button rather than a default. Frequent location updates keep the GPS radio awake, and an awake radio drinks. The default schedule and scheduled hours cover a normal day with battery to spare.",
          "halogps_17": "<strong>Default schedule.</strong> Periodic check-ins, easy on the battery.",
          "halogps_18": "<strong>Scheduled hours.</strong> Update more often on the walk to school and the walk home, less in between.",
          "halogps_19": "<strong>Active Tracking.</strong> Every 5 to 10 seconds, on demand.",
          "halogps_20": "SafeZones",
          "halogps_21": "A SafeZone is a circle you draw on the map. When your child crosses into it or out of it, you get a notification. Most families end up with three or four: home, school, practice, and a grandparent's house.",
          "halogps_22": "<strong>Arrival is the alert you will use most.</strong> \"Arrived at school\" at 8:12 a.m. answers the question you were about to ask.",
          "halogps_23": "<strong>Draw them a little generous.</strong> A zone drawn tight around a building fires more often than one that covers the block, because a GPS fix drifts a little even when your child is standing still.",
          "halogps_24": "<strong>HaloGPS makes them faster.</strong> Real-time SafeZone detection means the alert lands when they cross the line, not five minutes later.",
          "halogps_25": "Draw the circle, choose the alerts",
          "halogps_26": "How accurate it is, by situation",
          "halogps_27": "Accuracy is not one number. It changes with where your child is standing, and it helps to know the pattern before you read the map at 3 p.m. on a Tuesday.",
          "halogps_28": "Outdoors, open sky",
          "halogps_29": "The best case. Park, field, sidewalk, bike path. The dot lands on the right side of the street and stays there.",
          "halogps_30": "Dense or covered",
          "halogps_31": "Tall buildings, heavy tree cover, a parking garage. The fix holds, but expect the dot to settle for a moment.",
          "halogps_32": "Deep indoors",
          "halogps_33": "The middle of a school, a basement, a big-box store. Wi-Fi assist keeps you at the right building. Expect the building, not the room.",
          "halogps_34": "<b>Read the timestamp, not just the pin.</b> A location from eleven minutes ago is a different fact from one from eleven seconds ago. The app shows both. If you need the second, start Active Tracking.",
          "halogps_35": "Location history",
          "halogps_36": "The app keeps the day's trail, so \"where were they at 4?\" is something you can answer after the fact rather than something you had to be watching for. In practice it gets used for logistics far more than suspicion: confirming the bus ran late, or working out where a coat was left.",
          "halogps_37": "History follows the same accuracy rules as live location. A gap in the trail almost always means no signal or a dead battery.",
          "halogps_38": "Questions parents ask about location",
          "halogps_39": "Can I see where they are all day?",
          "halogps_40": "Yes. Location reports on the schedule you set, and the map is in the app whenever you open it. For a live view, Active Tracking updates every 5 to 10 seconds. For \"did they get there\", SafeZone arrival alerts do it without you opening the app at all.",
          "halogps_41": "Does location drain the battery?",
          "halogps_42": "Update frequency is the biggest factor in battery life by a wide margin. The default schedule gets a full day comfortably. Continuous Active Tracking will not. Use it for the stretches you need it, and let scheduled hours cover the rest. <a href=\"#battery\">More on battery</a>.",
          "halogps_43": "What if they take the watch off?",
          "halogps_44": "The map shows you where the watch is. There is no wear detection, so a watch left at a friend's house shows the friend's house. Watches that go on with shoes in the morning and come off at bedtime stay on wrists; it is a habit more than a feature.",
          "halogps_45": "What happens with no signal?",
          "halogps_46": "The map holds the last known fix with its timestamp, and catches up when signal returns. Texts sent in the meantime deliver when the watch reconnects, like any SMS.",
          "safecore_01": "Feature 02",
          "safecore_02": "SafeCore",
          "safecore_03": "The safety layer on every JrTrack. SOS to every Guardian with live location, SafeZone alerts, optional 911, and a contact list that keeps everyone else out.",
          "safecore_04": "<b>1 hold</b><span>to send an SOS</span>",
          "safecore_05": "<b>Every</b><span>Guardian alerted at once</span>",
          "safecore_06": "<b>911</b><span>optional, parent-enabled</span>",
          "safecore_07": "<b>0</b><span>unknown numbers get through</span>",
          "safecore_08": "What SafeCore includes",
          "safecore_09": "<em>In short</em>Four things, on every plan, with nothing behind a higher tier: SOS, SafeZones, optional 911, and approved contacts only.",
          "safecore_10": "SOS",
          "safecore_11": "A long hold on the side button alerts every parent and Guardian with the child's live location.",
          "safecore_12": "SafeZones",
          "safecore_13": "Arrival and departure alerts for the places you draw on the map. <a href=\"#safezones\">How they work</a>.",
          "safecore_14": "Optional 911",
          "safecore_15": "Off by default. You turn it on in the app when you decide your child is ready for it.",
          "safecore_16": "What happens when they hold the button",
          "safecore_17": "The sequence, start to finish.",
          "safecore_18": "Your child presses and holds the side button. On JrTrack Pro, that is the orange Action button.",
          "safecore_19": "The watch takes a fresh location fix rather than reusing the last known one.",
          "safecore_20": "Every Guardian on the account is alerted at once, not just the primary parent. Nobody has to be awake and holding their phone for the alert to reach the others.",
          "safecore_21": "The alert carries live location, so the map keeps updating while your child moves.",
          "safecore_22": "If you have enabled 911, your child can place that call from the same screen.",
          "safecore_23": "<b>SOS always gets through.</b> School-Fi, Bedtime, and every other restriction leave it on. There is no mode where the button does nothing.",
          "safecore_24": "One long hold, every Guardian notified",
          "safecore_25": "Guardians, and why you want more than one",
          "safecore_26": "A Guardian is an adult you invite into the account who can see location, receive SOS and SafeZone alerts, read messages, and change settings. A contact can only call and text. The most common setup gap is a single Guardian: one parent, one phone, one point of failure.",
          "safecore_27": "Add both parents, including across a split household. Alerts reach everyone at once.",
          "safecore_28": "Add the adult who is geographically closest during the school day. A grandparent five minutes away beats a parent forty minutes away.",
          "safecore_29": "The full Guardian-versus-contact breakdown is in <a href=\"#who-sees\">the Cosmo app chapter</a>.",
          "safecore_30": "More adults on the account, more coverage",
          "safecore_31": "911 calling",
          "safecore_32": "<em>In short</em>It is off until you turn it on, and you can turn it back off. That is deliberate.",
          "safecore_33": "A six-year-old with a 911 button on their wrist is a different proposition from an eleven-year-old who walks home alone. So the switch is yours, in the Cosmo app. Most families enable it around the time a child starts travelling a route on their own, after a conversation about what counts as an emergency and what happens on the other end of that call.",
          "safecore_34": "Approved contacts only",
          "safecore_35": "<em>In short</em>The watch only accepts calls and texts from numbers on the contact list, and only you can add to that list. Everything else is refused before it rings.",
          "safecore_36": "<strong>You add every contact,</strong> from the Cosmo app. Your child cannot add one from the watch.",
          "safecore_37": "<strong>Inbound is filtered.</strong> A wrong number, a spam call, a robotext, none of them reach the watch.",
          "safecore_38": "<strong>Outbound is limited the same way.</strong> The watch cannot dial a number that is not on the list.",
          "safecore_39": "<strong>There is no second door.</strong> No email, no social app, no browser, so nothing else to filter.",
          "safecore_40": "<b>Buddies are the one exception, and it still runs through you.</b> Kids can add each other as Buddies from the watch without swapping phone numbers, but every request waits for your approval. <a href=\"#buddies\">How Buddies work</a>.",
          "safecore_41": "Questions parents ask about safety",
          "safecore_42": "Is there someone on the other end of an SOS?",
          "safecore_43": "The alert goes to you and the Guardians you added, not to a Cosmo call center. That is why it pays to add more than one adult, and to include someone who is usually close to your child during the day. Optional 911 covers the case where a real emergency line is needed.",
          "safecore_44": "Can a stranger contact my child?",
          "safecore_45": "No. Inbound calls and texts are accepted only from the contact list, and only you can add to it. The watch also cannot call or text out to a number that is not on the list.",
          "safecore_46": "Does SOS work during School-Fi or Bedtime?",
          "safecore_47": "Yes. Every restriction level leaves SOS on. Location keeps reporting too.",
          "safecore_48": "Does it detect falls or monitor health?",
          "safecore_49": "No. JrTrack is a connection and location device, not a medical one. There is no fall detection or heart-rate alerting.",
          "texting_01": "Feature 03",
          "texting_02": "Texting",
          "texting_03": "TrueTexting gives the watch a real number and real SMS. Photos, videos, voice notes, group chats, and talk to text sit on top of it, and every word is readable in the Cosmo app.",
          "texting_04": "<b>SMS</b><span>from its own number</span>",
          "texting_05": "<b>MMS</b><span>photos, video, voice</span>",
          "texting_06": "<b>Groups</b><span>the whole family in one thread</span>",
          "texting_07": "TrueTexting: real SMS, from a real number",
          "texting_08": "<em>In short</em>The watch has its own phone number and sends normal texts. You message it from your phone's messaging app. No special app on your side, no account for grandma to create.",
          "texting_09": "Plenty of kids watches only talk to a proprietary app, which means every adult in your child's life has to download something, make an account, and remember it exists. TrueTexting skips all of that. A grandparent texts a number and it lands on the wrist.",
          "texting_10": "The watch also receives real calls the same way, over 4G LTE, in the US, Canada, and Mexico.<sup>3</sup>",
          "texting_11": "A real call, from a number you approved",
          "texting_12": "Photos, videos, voice notes, and group chats",
          "texting_13": "MMS that actually works. A picture from the field, a ten-second video, a quick voice note when typing is too slow. And one thread with Mom, Dad, and Grandma in it, where everyone is someone you approved.",
          "texting_14": "<strong>Photos and video.</strong> From the 5 MP front camera on JrTrack Pro, straight into a thread.",
          "texting_15": "<strong>Voice notes.</strong> Hold the mic when a sentence is too much to type.",
          "texting_16": "<strong>Group threads.</strong> The whole family in one conversation, every member approved by you.",
          "texting_17": "One thread for the whole family",
          "texting_18": "Talk it out, or type it",
          "texting_19": "<em>In short</em>Voice to text turns \"practice ran late, can you pick me up?\" into a real text before the keyboard even comes out. For the kid who is ready, there is a full keyboard sized for a wrist.",
          "texting_20": "A seven-year-old hunting for letters is the most common reason a kids watch stops getting used. Younger kids lean on the mic almost entirely for the first few months. That is normal.",
          "texting_21": "The keyboard has word suggestions, emoji, and a mic key. On the 1.78-inch OLED it is genuinely usable, even for a kid who has decided typing is a skill worth having.",
          "texting_22": "Keyboard, suggestions, emoji, and a mic key",
          "texting_23": "Every message, readable in the Cosmo app",
          "texting_24": "Texts, photos, and voice notes all show up on your phone as they happen. Texting with training wheels: your child learns to communicate on a device where the whole thread is visible to a parent and the contact list cannot include a stranger.",
          "texting_25": "Your child sees the same messages on their own wrist. This is a visible watch, not hidden software, and families who frame it as \"how we stay in touch when you are out on your own\" get a device that gets worn.",
          "texting_26": "The same thread, on your phone",
          "texting_27": "Questions parents ask about messaging",
          "texting_28": "Do relatives need the Cosmo app to text the watch?",
          "texting_29": "No. Anyone you add as a contact texts the watch's number from their normal messaging app. Only Guardians use the Cosmo app, and only for the management side.",
          "texting_30": "Can it video call?",
          "texting_31": "Not currently. The front camera is for photos and short videos sent into a thread. Voice calls work with every approved contact.",
          "texting_32": "Can my child text someone I have not approved?",
          "texting_33": "No. Outbound texts and calls are limited to the contact list the same way inbound ones are.",
          "texting_34": "Does texting work in Canada or Mexico?",
          "texting_35": "Texting works in the US and Canada. Calling works in the US, Canada, and Mexico.<sup>3</sup>"
        }
      },
      "jrtrack-wiki-part-4": {
        "type": "jrtrack-wiki-part-4",
        "settings": {
          "schoolfi_01": "Feature 04",
          "schoolfi_02": "School-Fi&#8482;",
          "schoolfi_03": "Recognizes the campus and goes distraction-free from bell to bell, on its own. No schedule to manage, nothing for your child to remember. SOS always gets through.",
          "schoolfi_04": "<b>Any</b><span>school campus in America</span>",
          "schoolfi_05": "<b>3</b><span>restriction levels</span>",
          "schoolfi_06": "<b>Auto</b><span>holidays, half days, snow days</span>",
          "schoolfi_07": "How it works",
          "schoolfi_08": "<em>In short</em>School-Fi is triggered by location, not by a calendar. The moment they step on campus the watch goes quiet at the level you chose. Off campus, everything switches back on by itself.",
          "schoolfi_09": "Schedule-based school modes break on exactly the days you forget about: holidays, half days, snow days, early dismissal, a field trip that runs late. Because School-Fi is powered by HaloGPS, none of those need managing. It knows where school is, so it knows when it is school.",
          "schoolfi_10": "You get the arrival notification when they get there, and the watch handles the rest of the day.",
          "schoolfi_11": "\"Emma's School routine has started\"",
          "schoolfi_12": "Set it once",
          "schoolfi_13": "<strong>Choose their school.</strong> Search any school in America and adjust the automatic geofence to fit the campus.",
          "schoolfi_14": "<strong>Customize essentials.</strong> Pick the restriction level and your notification preferences.",
          "schoolfi_15": "<strong>Let JrTrack handle the rest.</strong> Holidays, new schedules, snow days. It keeps up on its own.",
          "schoolfi_16": "<b>The geofence</b>Drawn around the campus, adjustable to fit",
          "schoolfi_17": "<b>The routine</b>Restriction level and notifications, in one screen",
          "schoolfi_18": "Fully, partially, or unrestricted. Your call.",
          "schoolfi_19": "Fully restricted",
          "schoolfi_20": "No calls or texts in",
          "schoolfi_21": "No apps or games",
          "schoolfi_22": "SOS still works",
          "schoolfi_23": "Location still reports",
          "schoolfi_24": "Partially restricted",
          "schoolfi_25": "Parents can still get through",
          "schoolfi_26": "No apps or games",
          "schoolfi_27": "SOS still works",
          "schoolfi_28": "Location still reports",
          "schoolfi_29": "Unrestricted",
          "schoolfi_30": "Everything stays on",
          "schoolfi_31": "Apps available",
          "schoolfi_32": "SOS still works",
          "schoolfi_33": "Location still reports",
          "schoolfi_34": "Most families start fully restricted for the first term. It is the setting that makes a teacher stop noticing the watch, which is the outcome you want.",
          "schoolfi_35": "What the school day looks like",
          "schoolfi_36": "<b>Arriving on campus</b>The watch goes distraction-free on its own, and you get the arrival alert.",
          "schoolfi_37": "<b>During class</b>Fully, partially, or unrestricted. SOS always gets through.",
          "schoolfi_38": "<b>On the way home</b>Off campus, everything switches back on by itself.",
          "schoolfi_39": "<b>Away-for-the-day schools</b>The School Clip carries JrTrack on a backpack so it stays connected.",
          "schoolfi_40": "The School Clip",
          "schoolfi_41": "Some schools have away-for-the-day policies where wrist devices go in a pouch or a locker. The School Clip snaps JrTrack onto a backpack or belt loop instead of a wrist, so it stays with your child and stays connected on the walk to and from school. Wear it, clip it, you decide.",
          "schoolfi_42": "School-Fi runs the same way either way, because it keys off location, not off how the watch is worn.",
          "schoolfi_43": "Sold separately",
          "schoolfi_44": "Questions parents ask about school",
          "schoolfi_45": "Will the school allow it?",
          "schoolfi_46": "Policies vary and change year to year, so check yours. \"Silent and out of sight during class\" is satisfied by fully restricted School-Fi. \"No phones, watches allowed\" is the most common policy and the one JrTrack was designed around. Away-for-the-day policies are what the School Clip is for.",
          "schoolfi_47": "Parents who explain to the teacher in week one that the watch is locked to a handful of contacts and goes silent on campus almost never have a problem.",
          "schoolfi_48": "What about a school that is not in the database?",
          "schoolfi_49": "School-Fi recognizes any school campus in America, and the geofence is adjustable, so an unusual campus layout can be drawn to fit.",
          "schoolfi_50": "Can I still reach them during class?",
          "schoolfi_51": "On partially restricted, parents still get through. On fully restricted, nothing rings during class, but SOS still works and location still reports. Most families pick fully restricted and use the arrival alert as their check-in.",
          "routines_01": "Feature 05",
          "routines_02": "Routines",
          "routines_03": "School, bedtime, practice, homework, Sunday dinner. Build a routine for any time or place and the watch runs it without anyone remembering.",
          "routines_04": "<b>School</b><span>by location</span>",
          "routines_05": "<b>Bedtime</b><span>by hours</span>",
          "routines_06": "<b>Yours</b><span>by either</span>",
          "routines_07": "Routines that run themselves",
          "routines_08": "<em>In short</em>A routine is a set of watch rules that starts on its own, by schedule or by location. School-Fi is one. Bedtime is another. You can build the rest.",
          "routines_09": "<strong>School.</strong> Fully, partially, or unrestricted while they are on campus. Powered by School-Fi.",
          "routines_10": "<strong>Bedtime.</strong> Lights out means screen off. Set the hours once, and calls from parents still ring.",
          "routines_11": "<strong>Your routine.</strong> Practice, homework, Sunday dinner. Pick a time or a place, choose what gets through, and tune how often location updates while it runs.",
          "routines_12": "Every routine leaves SOS on and location reporting. The only things a routine can turn off are the things that distract.",
          "routines_13": "Set it once, it runs on its own",
          "routines_14": "What they see on the wrist",
          "routines_15": "A routine announces itself before it starts, so nobody is surprised mid-sentence. The watch face changes, the app grid trims down, and the things you left on stay exactly where they were.",
          "routines_16": "<strong>School</strong> starts on arrival and ends on departure.",
          "routines_17": "<strong>Bedtime</strong> turns the screen off, and parents still ring.",
          "routines_18": "<strong>A heads-up</strong> shows a few minutes before any routine kicks in.",
          "routines_19": "The watch gives a heads-up before it changes",
          "appstation_01": "Feature 06",
          "appstation_02": "App Station &amp; music",
          "appstation_03": "A curated, kid-safe library. You approve each app from your phone, with time limits if you want them. Spotify, Spotify Kids, and iHeartRadio on the wrist or through Bluetooth headphones.",
          "appstation_04": "<b>You</b><span>install every app</span>",
          "appstation_05": "<b>Daily</b><span>time limits per app</span>",
          "appstation_06": "<b>3</b><span>music services</span>",
          "appstation_07": "How App Station works",
          "appstation_08": "<em>In short</em>You choose from the library in the Cosmo app and install to the watch. Your child cannot add anything themselves, and there is no store on the wrist to browse.",
          "appstation_09": "Each app you approve can carry a daily time limit, set in the app rather than negotiated on the wrist. When the limit is up, the app closes and the rest of the watch keeps working.",
          "appstation_10": "The library grows. New apps land in App Station, and nothing appears on your child's watch until you put it there.",
          "appstation_11": "Install and set limits from your phone",
          "appstation_12": "The full library",
          "appstation_13": "<em>In short</em>This is the complete list. Every app that can go on the watch is here, and nothing outside it can be installed.",
          "appstation_14": "<b>Music</b><span>Spotify, Spotify Kids, iHeartRadio</span>",
          "appstation_15": "<b>Puzzle games</b><span>2048, Flappy Bot, Swip3</span>",
          "appstation_16": "<b>Health and wellness</b><span>Calm, GoNoodle, Action Hero</span>",
          "appstation_17": "<b>Productivity</b><span>Simple Checklist</span>",
          "appstation_18": "<b>Learning</b><span>The Week Junior, Word of the Day</span>",
          "appstation_19": "<b>Standard on the watch</b><span>Weather, calculator, activity tracking, camera, wallpapers, stopwatch, alarms</span>",
          "appstation_20": "<b>My apps</b>Every app is one you approved, laid out two by two so small fingers hit the right one",
          "appstation_21": "<b>Music</b>A playlist for the bus and a soundtrack for the walk home, over Wi-Fi or Bluetooth headphones",
          "appstation_22": "No browser, no store, no social",
          "appstation_23": "<em>In short</em>There is no web browser, no app store, no social media, and no YouTube on the watch. Not restricted or filtered. Absent.",
          "appstation_24": "This is the architectural decision behind the whole product. A device with a browser is a device where every other protection can be walked around. Leaving it out is what makes \"you approve every app\" a real statement rather than a setting.",
          "appstation_25": "Music is the one streaming exception, and it runs through the services above over Wi-Fi.",
          "appstation_26": "Questions parents ask about apps",
          "appstation_27": "Will this become another screen to fight about?",
          "appstation_28": "It can, if the app list grows without limits. Families who have run one for a year suggest starting with almost nothing: messages, calls, and a watch face. Add the first game after a month, use daily limits from day one, and let Bedtime handle the nightstand.",
          "appstation_29": "Can my child download something without me knowing?",
          "appstation_30": "No. Installation happens only from the Cosmo app on your phone. There is no store on the watch.",
          "appstation_31": "Does music use the cellular plan?",
          "appstation_32": "Music streams over Wi-Fi. Every plan includes unlimited data, so there is no overage to watch either way.",
          "app_01": "Feature 07",
          "app_02": "The Cosmo app",
          "app_03": "Your all-in-one hub for location, contacts, routines, apps, and every message on the watch. More confidence for you, so freedom for them comes with peace of mind.",
          "app_04": "<b>iOS</b><span>and Android</span>",
          "app_05": "<b>Every</b><span>message, readable</span>",
          "app_06": "<b>Live</b><span>map, always</span>",
          "app_07": "Everything in the Cosmo app",
          "app_08": "<em>In short</em>If the watch is the product your child uses, the app is the product you use. Nothing on the watch is set from the watch.",
          "app_09": "<b>Read along, anytime</b>Every text and photo on the watch is viewable in the app.",
          "app_10": "<b>Approve apps, set limits</b>Choose from kid-safe apps and manage daily time limits.",
          "app_11": "<b>Tracking that fits your day</b>Update frequency, scheduled hours, or Active Tracking on demand.",
          "app_12": "Guardians, contacts, and Buddies",
          "app_13": "Three roles, and the difference between them answers most privacy questions.",
          "app_15": "Guardian",
          "app_16": "Contact",
          "app_17": "Buddy",
          "app_18": "Call and text the watch",
          "app_19": "Yes",
          "app_20": "Yes",
          "app_21": "Yes, watch to watch",
          "app_22": "See live location",
          "app_23": "Yes",
          "app_24": "No",
          "app_25": "No",
          "app_26": "Receive SOS alerts",
          "app_27": "Yes",
          "app_28": "No",
          "app_29": "No",
          "app_30": "Read messages in the app",
          "app_31": "Yes",
          "app_32": "No",
          "app_33": "No",
          "app_34": "Change settings and routines",
          "app_35": "Yes",
          "app_36": "No",
          "app_37": "No",
          "app_38": "Added by",
          "app_39": "You",
          "app_40": "You",
          "app_41": "Your child, approved by you",
          "app_42": "A grandparent you add as a contact can talk to your child all day and will never see a map. Making them a Guardian is a separate, deliberate act.",
          "app_43": "What Cosmo holds",
          "app_44": "Running the service means holding some data: the account, location so the map can show it, messages so they can be delivered and read in the app, and the diagnostics that keep a fleet of watches working.",
          "app_45": "Your child's data is not sold, and the watch carries no advertising.",
          "app_46": "There is no third-party ad network on the device, because there is no browser and no ad-supported app.",
          "app_47": "The account belongs to the parent. Deleting it removes the child profile with it.",
          "app_48": "<b>For the exact terms,</b> including retention and deletion requests, the <a href=\"/pages/privacy-policy\">Cosmo privacy policy</a> is the document that governs. This page is the plain-language version.",
          "app_49": "Tap through the Cosmo app",
          "app_50": "A working copy of the parent app: the live map, contacts, routines, App Station, and every message. Right here in your browser.",
          "app_51": "Try the app",
          "mobile_01": "Feature 08",
          "mobile_02": "Cosmo Mobile",
          "mobile_03": "Coverage that keeps up. A FlexSIM eSIM that roams across major network towers for reliable 4G LTE, with unlimited talk, text, and data on every plan.<sup>3</sup>",
          "mobile_04": "<b>4G LTE</b><span>FlexSIM, pre-installed</span>",
          "mobile_05": "<b>3</b><span>countries for calling</span>",
          "mobile_06": "<b>From $9.99</b><span>per month</span>",
          "mobile_07": "How FlexSIM works",
          "mobile_08": "<em>In short</em>An eSIM is already inside the watch. It roams across major network towers, including AT&amp;T and US Cellular, and takes whichever signal is strongest. Nothing to install, no carrier to call.",
          "mobile_09": "Because it roams rather than sitting on a single network, coverage tends to hold in places where one carrier alone is thin. Calling works in the US, Canada, and Mexico; texting in the US and Canada.<sup>3</sup> Apps, music, and updates use Wi-Fi when it is available, which takes load off cellular at home.",
          "mobile_10": "Activation happens in the Cosmo app right out of the box. Wi-Fi features work as soon as the watch is paired; cellular can take up to 24 hours to fully come online, so set up on an evening or a weekend rather than a school morning.",
          "mobile_11": "The plans",
          "mobile_12": "Every plan includes the same thing: unlimited talk, text, and data, full Cosmo app access, 24/7 GPS, and a 30-day money-back guarantee. The only difference is how long you commit and what that lowers the monthly to. Choose during activation, upgrade any time.",
          "mobile_13": "Plan",
          "mobile_14": "Monthly equivalent",
          "mobile_15": "Paid",
          "mobile_16": "Notes",
          "mobile_17": "Try it out",
          "mobile_18": "$19.99/mo",
          "mobile_19": "Monthly",
          "mobile_20": "No commitment, cancel any time",
          "mobile_21": "6-Month",
          "mobile_22": "$14.99/mo",
          "mobile_23": "$89.94 up front",
          "mobile_25": "1-Year",
          "mobile_26": "$12.99/mo",
          "mobile_27": "$155.88 up front",
          "mobile_28": "Most popular",
          "mobile_29": "2-Year",
          "mobile_30": "$9.99/mo",
          "mobile_31": "$239.76 up front",
          "mobile_32": "Lowest monthly",
          "mobile_33": "<b>All new lines require a one-time $14.99 activation fee.</b> Prices in USD.",
          "mobile_34": "Questions parents ask about the plan",
          "mobile_35": "Can I add it to my own carrier's family plan?",
          "mobile_36": "No. JrTrack works only with a Cosmo Mobile plan. There is no SIM tray, and the watch cannot be added to a Verizon, AT&amp;T, or T-Mobile family plan. FlexSIM is what lets it roam across networks instead of being locked to one.",
          "mobile_37": "Is there a contract?",
          "mobile_38": "Try it out is month to month with no commitment. The 6-month, 1-year, and 2-year plans are prepaid for the term in exchange for a lower monthly. Every plan carries the 30-day money-back guarantee.",
          "mobile_39": "What happens if we cancel?",
          "mobile_40": "Inside 30 days, the money-back guarantee applies. After that, Try it out can be cancelled any time, and prepaid terms run to the end of the term. Billing lives in the Parent Portal.",
          "mobile_41": "Does it work outside the US?",
          "mobile_42": "Calling covers the US, Canada, and Mexico. Texting covers the US and Canada.<sup>3</sup> Beyond those, treat the watch as a Wi-Fi device."
        }
      },
      "jrtrack-wiki-part-5": {
        "type": "jrtrack-wiki-part-5",
        "settings": {
          "design_01": "Feature 09",
          "design_02": "Design &amp; battery",
          "design_03": "Built to keep up. The slimmest JrTrack yet, a 1.78-inch OLED, a 720 mAh cell, and a frame made for playgrounds and bike racks.<sup>†</sup>",
          "design_04": "<b>12.8 mm</b><span>front to back</span>",
          "design_05": "<b>720 mAh</b><span>24+ hours<sup>1</sup></span>",
          "design_06": "<b>3 ATM</b><span>water resistant<sup>2</sup></span>",
          "design_07": "<b>~90 min</b><span>to full charge</span>",
          "design_08": "A closer look at JrTrack Pro",
          "design_09": "<b>Slimmest build yet</b>12.8 mm front to back, so it sits flat on a small wrist instead of standing off it.",
          "design_10": "<b>1.78-inch OLED</b>A 368 by 488 panel fills the front. Deeper blacks, brighter colour, and room for a full keyboard.",
          "design_11": "<b>Action button</b>A press opens the shortcut you choose. A long hold sends an SOS with live location to every Guardian.",
          "design_12": "<b>Reinforced frame</b>A stiffer body and scratch-resistant glass,<sup>†</sup> built for the bottom of a backpack.",
          "design_13": "<b>Charging contacts</b>Four gold contacts meet the magnetic charger, so it lands the right way round every time.",
          "design_14": "<b>Quick-swap bands</b>Soft silicone with a proper buckle, and pins that let them change the look in seconds.",
          "design_15": "All-day battery, and then some",
          "design_16": "<em>In short</em>A 720 mAh cell. 24+ hours at default settings and about 90 minutes to full on the magnetic charger.<sup>1</sup> Bus stop to bedtime, with room to spare.",
          "design_17": "Battery complaints about kids watches are almost always a settings story. The things that move the needle, in order:",
          "design_18": "<strong>Location update frequency.</strong> By far the biggest factor. Continuous Active Tracking will cut runtime hard; the default schedule will not.",
          "design_19": "<strong>Weak signal.</strong> A watch hunting for a tower works harder than one sitting on a strong one.",
          "design_20": "<strong>Screen time and music.</strong> Games and streaming over Wi-Fi cost real battery.",
          "design_21": "<strong>Cold.</strong> A January bus stop takes a bite out of any lithium cell.",
          "design_22": "The habit that settles it: a charging stand on the nightstand, and the Bedtime routine. Ninety minutes covers a full charge, and they are asleep far longer than that.",
          "design_23": "Drop it on at night, ready by morning",
          "design_24": "Water, and what 3 ATM covers",
          "design_25": "<em>In short</em>JrTrack Pro is rated 3 ATM, which is 30 meters or about 98 feet of static pressure.<sup>2</sup> That covers rain, sinks, sprinklers, and puddles. It is not a swim or dive rating.",
          "design_26": "\"30 meters\" is a pressure test in a lab, not a depth you can take it to. Jumping into a pool creates far more force than standing still under 30 meters of water, which is why no watch at this rating should go off a diving board.",
          "design_27": "Fine",
          "design_28": "Rain, sleet, and snow",
          "design_29": "Handwashing and sinks",
          "design_30": "Sprinklers and water balloons",
          "design_31": "A puddle they did not see",
          "design_32": "Take it off",
          "design_33": "Swimming and pool days",
          "design_34": "Diving and water slides",
          "design_35": "Baths and long showers",
          "design_36": "Hot tubs, where heat is the real problem",
          "design_37": "<b>Kids will get it wet anyway.</b> Water damage is covered from the basic Cosmo Care tier. <a href=\"#cosmo-care\">How Cosmo Care works</a>.",
          "design_38": "Made for real kid life",
          "design_39": "A reinforced frame, scratch-resistant glass,<sup>†</sup> and two screen covers in the box, which tells you what we expect to happen. Playgrounds, bike racks, and the bottom of a backpack are the design brief, not the edge case.",
          "design_40": "What still breaks: a screen that meets concrete corner-first, and a watch that never came home from camp. Neither is a warranty claim, which is the entire reason Cosmo Care exists.",
          "design_41": "Both a silicone band and a velcro band ship in the box. Velcro is usually the answer for the youngest wrists. Bands swap in seconds, so a band that stops fitting is a $10.99 problem, not a new watch.",
          "design_42": "Designed for the way this actually goes",
          "design_43": "Full specifications, JrTrack Pro",
          "design_44": "<b>Dimensions</b><span>52 × 43 × 12.8 mm<sup>†</sup></span>",
          "design_45": "<b>Display</b><span>1.78-inch OLED touchscreen, 368 × 488, scratch resistant<sup>†</sup></span>",
          "design_46": "<b>Battery</b><span>720 mAh. 24+ hours at default settings, about 90 minutes to full<sup>1</sup></span>",
          "design_47": "<b>Memory</b><span>32 GB storage, 3 GB RAM</span>",
          "design_48": "<b>Camera</b><span>5 MP front-facing, photos and short videos to approved contacts</span>",
          "design_49": "<b>Water resistance</b><span>3 ATM, water rated to 30 m (98 ft)<sup>2</sup></span>",
          "design_50": "<b>Connectivity</b><span>4G LTE, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth 4.2, NFC for tagging</span>",
          "design_51": "<b>Sensors</b><span>Activity, direction, and altitude</span>",
          "design_52": "<b>Cellular</b><span>FlexSIM with eSIM pre-installed. Cosmo Mobile only.</span>",
          "design_53": "<b>Location</b><span>HaloGPS, 115 satellite signals, Active Tracking, Wi-Fi assist</span>",
          "design_54": "<b>Safety</b><span>SafeCore: SOS to all Guardians, SafeZones, optional 911, approved contacts only</span>",
          "design_55": "<b>Coverage</b><span>Calling in US, Canada, Mexico. Texting in US and Canada.<sup>3</sup></span>",
          "design_56": "<b>In the box</b><span>Watch, charging clip and wall adapter, 2 screen covers, silicone and velcro bands, quickstart guide</span>",
          "care_01": "Feature 10",
          "care_02": "Cosmo Care &amp; guarantees",
          "care_03": "Kids will be kids. We planned for that. Optional protection for the watch itself, plus the guarantees that come with buying from the official Cosmo store.",
          "care_04": "<b>30 days</b><span>money back</span>",
          "care_05": "<b>3 years</b><span>warranty</span>",
          "care_06": "<b>From $2.99</b><span>per month, Cosmo Care</span>",
          "care_07": "How Cosmo Care works",
          "care_08": "<em>In short</em>Optional protection for the watch, billed monthly, no annual plan, no tax. A cracked screen, a swim it was not supposed to take, or a watch that never came home: one short call to support and a replacement is on its way.",
          "care_10": "Cosmo Care Basic, $2.99/mo",
          "care_11": "Cosmo Care Plus, $5.99/mo",
          "care_12": "Accidental damage",
          "care_13": "Yes",
          "care_14": "Yes",
          "care_15": "Water damage",
          "care_16": "Yes",
          "care_17": "Yes",
          "care_18": "Free replacements",
          "care_19": "One per year",
          "care_20": "One per year",
          "care_21": "Loss and theft",
          "care_22": "No",
          "care_23": "Yes",
          "care_24": "Annual upgrade to the latest device",
          "care_25": "No",
          "care_26": "Yes",
          "care_27": "If your child has never lost a jacket, Basic is probably enough. If \"it was in my backpack\" is a regular feature of your week, Plus covers loss, and the annual upgrade takes some of the sting out of the monthly. Add it at checkout.",
          "care_28": "“Thankfully we bought the insurance, because she lost her watch this month. After a short call to the support team, her replacement arrived in three days with no issues.” <cite>Chloie, verified buyer</cite>",
          "care_29": "The guarantees",
          "care_30": "<b>Money-back guarantee</b><span>30 days, on every membership</span>",
          "care_31": "<b>Warranty</b><span>Three years, on manufacturing defects</span>",
          "care_32": "<b>Free upgrades</b><span>Every two years, buying from the official Cosmo store</span>",
          "care_33": "<b>Support</b><span>Real humans, seven days a week</span>",
          "care_34": "<b>Made in</b><span>Pennsylvania. Available in the US and Canada</span>",
          "care_35": "<b>Buy from cosmotogether.com.</b> The guarantee, the warranty, and the two-year upgrade apply to watches bought from the official store.",
          "care_36": "What happens if",
          "care_37": "The watch is lost",
          "care_38": "Check the map first. The last known location and its timestamp are in the app, and a watch with battery and signal keeps reporting. Most \"lost\" watches are located this way within minutes.",
          "care_39": "If it is genuinely gone, Cosmo Care Plus covers loss and theft. Basic does not.",
          "care_40": "The screen is cracked",
          "care_41": "Accidental damage is covered by both Cosmo Care tiers, with one free replacement per year. Without Cosmo Care, a drop is not a warranty claim; the three-year warranty covers manufacturing defects.",
          "care_42": "My child outgrows it",
          "care_43": "Most families get two to four years out of a JrTrack before a phone conversation starts in earnest. Bands stop fitting first, and those swap for around $10.99. If a younger sibling is coming up behind, the watch moves down with a new profile. Cosmo Care Plus includes a free annual upgrade to the latest device.",
          "care_44": "We are a split household",
          "care_45": "Add both parents as Guardians. Alerts, location, and messages reach both at once, and neither has to relay anything. One account, one watch, and the app is the shared surface.",
          "care_46": "We want to return it",
          "care_47": "Every membership comes with a 30-day money-back guarantee, so the first month is genuinely a trial. At week four, ask honestly whether it gets worn and used. If not, say so and return it.",
          "grows_01": "Feature 11",
          "grows_02": "The watch that grows up",
          "grows_03": "Buddies, watch faces, steps and streaks. The parts of JrTrack that are theirs, with you still holding the switch.",
          "grows_04": "Buddies",
          "grows_05": "<em>In short</em>Kids add each other as Buddies right from the watch, no phone numbers to swap. You approve every one before it goes live.",
          "grows_06": "Two kids with JrTracks hold their watches together, tap to add, and the request lands in each parent's Cosmo app. Nothing connects until both parents say yes. After that, Buddies can message each other watch to watch, and their step counts show up on a friendly leaderboard.",
          "grows_07": "It is the one door your child can open, and it still needs your key.",
          "grows_08": "Tap to add, parent approves",
          "grows_09": "Watch faces, steps, and streaks",
          "grows_10": "<strong>Watch faces.</strong> Digital, analog, or pared back, in eight accent colours and ten wallpapers. Their photo, their colors, their watch, and it changes in seconds.",
          "grows_11": "<strong>Steps and streaks.</strong> A daily goal that fills a ring, and a friendly leaderboard with approved Buddies. Progress shows up in the Cosmo app too.",
          "grows_12": "<strong>Bands.</strong> Quick-swap pins, so the look changes as fast as they change their minds.",
          "grows_13": "A face on the 1.78-inch OLED",
          "compare_01": "Which JrTrack is right for you?",
          "compare_02": "Pro, 5 SE, or 5 Renewed",
          "compare_03": "<em>In short</em>HaloGPS, SafeCore, TrueTexting, School-Fi, and App Station are on all three. Pro adds a slimmer body, a much better display, a bigger battery, and a better camera.",
          "compare_05": "JrTrack Pro",
